)HELP" METAL_HELP_FULL_TARGET_TYPES_FOR_C R"HELP([!]
[!] With -c, at least one target type must be selected. Multiple types are allowed.
[!]
[!] Default -c (if not set): cus

[!] [!] FILTERS [!]
[!] -bf PATH                        Bloom filter(s).
[!] -xu PATH                        XOR uncompressed filter(s).
[!] -xc PATH                        XOR compressed filter(s).
[!] -xuc PATH                       XOR ultra-compressed filter(s).
[!] -xh PATH                        XOR HC filter(s).
[!] -hash HEX                       Fast target prefix match (2..32 bytes, 4..64 hex chars);
[!]                                 full 32-byte payload targets are for XP direct outputs.
[!] -target HEX                     Alias for -hash. In -profanity -recovery this must be a 33/65-byte pubkey.
[!] -xx PATH                        Optional extra CPU XOR Uncompressed verification.
[!] -xb PATH                        Extra CPU Bloom verification.
[!] -full                           Debug: treat all hashes as found. (carefully!)

[!] [!] CURVE / GPU PARAMS [!]
[!] -em                             Enable secp256k1 endomorphism (6x compressed checks per EC point).
[!]                                 Default: off. Use -em -priv seq or random mode.
[!] -bit N                          secp256k1 precompute bits 1..24.
[!]                                 Default auto: 18 for -priv/-minikeys, 16 for others.
[!] -scalar                         Treat 32-byte PVK as ed25519 scalar.
[!]     -LE                         With -scalar: scalar input is little-endian.
[!] -shash                          Treat 32-byte PVK as ed25519 hash(seed pre-clamp).
[!] -device LIST                    Metal device list, e.g. 0 or 0,1,3 or 0-3.
[!] -b N                            Blocks.
[!] -t N                            Threads per block.
[!] -fsize N                        Founds array size per round.
[!] -ada-pointer slot:tx:cert       Add Shelley pointer address metadata.

[!] NOTE: for -priv/-minikeys/-brain with -round >= 10 and no -b/-t,
[!] blocks are auto reduced to 128.

[!] ======================================================================
[!] GLOBAL ITERATION / PASSWORD / RANDOM / SEQ FLAGS
[!] ======================================================================
[!] -iter LIST                      Iterations list/range, example: 1,4,6-10.
[!] -utf8                           Use hash output as UTF-8 hex text for next iter.
[!] -text                           Use hash output as text payload (mode-dependent).
[!] -round N                        Run +-N rounds for every private key.
[!] -pbkdf N                        PBKDF2 iterations (default 2048).
[!] -lang EN|SA|JA|IT|FR|CZ|PT|KO|CS|CT
[!] -w N                            Word count helper for random generation.
[!] -size N                         Custom entropy size bytes.
[!] -sizes LIST                     Multiple sizes, example: 16,24,32.
[!] -hexset HEXDIGITS               Forward-only mixed-radix seq from user hex nibble set.
[!]                                 Accepts 1..16 unique hex digits, preserves user order.
[!]                                 Use with -start HEX [-end HEX].
[!]                                 -start is required, -end is optional.
[!]                                 Bounds must be even-length hex strings.
[!]                                 Different lengths mean length-major scan between them.
[!]                                 Incompatible with -size/-sizes/-step/-endstep/-plusstep/
[!]                                 -addplusstep/-back/-both/-random/-n/-prng/-prng64/
[!]                                 -pass_thread/-der_thread.
[!]                                 Example:
[!]                                 ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -seed -hexset abc0 -start 00 -end ffffffff -d derivations.txt -c c
[!] -MODE -recovery TEMPLATE -hexset HEXDIGITS
[!]                                 Mode-scoped byte/hex recovery on GPU.
[!]                                 Flags are order-independent: -recovery may appear before or after -MODE.
[!]                                 TEMPLATE accepts hex chars and '*'.
[!]                                 '*' = one hex nibble generated from -hexset in user order.
[!]                                 -recovery -i FILE is supported; one hex template per line.
[!]                                 Candidate length/min_len are fixed by each TEMPLATE length.
[!]                                 Do not use -start/-end/-size/-sizes with this branch.
[!]                                 Supported: -mnemonic, -entropy, -seed, -hmac, -bip32,
[!]                                 -brain, -old, -old -seed, -armory, -root.
[!]                                 Examples:
[!]                                 ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -seed -recovery 0* -hexset abc -d derivations.txt -c c
[!]                                 ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -recovery 0* -hexset abc -seed -d derivations.txt -c c
[!]                                 ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -seed -recovery -i seed_templates.txt -hexset abc -d derivations.txt -c c
[!]                                 ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -entropy -recovery 000*0000*000* -hexset 0abcdf -d derivations.txt -c c
[!]
[!] [!] BIP39 PASSPHRASE FLAGS [!]
[!] -pass FILE|PASSWORD             File path -> many passphrases, otherwise 1 password.
[!] -passbrute HEX:HEX              Brute passphrase range in HEX.
[!] -pass_thread                    Invert mode: one mnemonic per round, passwords split across threads/GPUs.
[!] -der_thread                     Invert mode: one input per round, derivation paths split across GPU threads.

[!] [!] SEQUENTIAL FLAGS [!]
[!] -start VALUE                    Start point (enables seq mode).
[!] -end VALUE                      End point.
[!] -step HEX                       Step (hex), default 1.
[!] -endstep HEX                    End step for seq step-schedule mode. (-priv only)
[!] -plusstep NUMBER                Add to step for next schedule round. (-priv only)
[!] -addplusstep NUMBER             Add to plusstep after endstep wrap. (-priv only)
[!] -back                           Backward seq branch.
[!] -both                           Without -end: walk both directions around -start.
[!]                                 With -start/-end: + starts at -start, - starts at -end.
[!] -random                         Random branch (standalone or seq-random).
[!] -n N                            Seq-random span override.
[!] -mask MASK                      Wallet candidate mask, e.g. ?l?l?l?d?d.
[!] -mask-file FILE                 Wallet masks file, one mask per line.
[!] -cs1 CHARS                      Custom wallet mask charset used by ?1.
[!] -cs2 CHARS                      Custom wallet mask charset used by ?2.
[!] -cs3 CHARS                      Custom wallet mask charset used by ?3.
[!] -cs4 CHARS                      Custom wallet mask charset used by ?4.

[!] [!] SEQ POINT LENGTH / FORMAT [!]
[!] -priv: 64
[!] -minikeys: mini key string "S..." (length 22/26/30, base58)
[!] -minikeys -seed: HEX seed point (up to 128 hex chars = 64 bytes)
[!] -seed/-hmac: 128
[!] -brain: 512
[!] -mnemonic/-entropy/-bip32/-old: 1024

[!] ======================================================================
[!] MAIN MODE: -keystore  (Ethereum JSON V3 password recovery)
[!] ======================================================================
[!] Purpose:
[!] Recover passwords for Ethereum JSON V3 keystore files on GPU.
[!] Production worker supports PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 and scrypt + AES-128-CTR.
[!] Scrypt support is GPU-side for r<=8 and allocates per-candidate scratch.

[!] Enable:
[!] -keystore FILE                 Load one JSON keystore.
[!] -keystore -f DIR               Recursively load JSON V3 keystores from directory.
[!]                                Scans *.json/*.key/*.keystore/UTC--*/UTC_* and no-extension JSON.
[!]                                Also scans *.txt/*.hash for hashcat
[!]                                $ethereum$s*N*r*p*salt*ciphertext*mac lines.

[!] Candidate sources:
[!] -i FILE                        Dictionary/password list, repeatable.
[!] -hex                           Dictionary lines are hex-encoded password bytes.
[!] -mask MASK                     GPU mask brute force.
[!] -mask-file FILE                One mask per line.
[!] -start HEX [-end HEX]          Raw byte range, length-major, up to 127 bytes.
[!] -n N                           Optional launch concurrency cap for scrypt.
[!]                                Default is capped at 64 active scrypt candidates
[!]                                to avoid wasting VRAM on high-N keystores.
[!]                                Increase -n manually if you want more scratch/VRAM.

[!] Save format:
[!] KEYSTORE:<file>:PASSWORD:<password>:PRIV:<private64hex>:ETH:<0xaddress>
[!] KEYSTORE:<file#line>:PASSWORD:<password>:VAULT:<sha256>:PROFILE:web3-secret-storage-mac

[!] Examples:
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -keystore wallet.json -i passwords.txt -save
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -keystore -f keystores -mask "?a?a?a?a?a?a" -save

[!] ======================================================================
[!] MAIN MODE: -walletdat  (Bitcoin Core wallet.dat password recovery)
[!] ======================================================================
[!] Purpose:
[!] Recover Bitcoin Core wallet.dat passwords on GPU.
[!] One candidate stream is checked against all loaded wallet targets.
[!] Multiple wallet.dat files are supported in one run.
[!] Parser supports Bitcoin Core BerkeleyDB marker scan for mkey/ckey records,
[!] raw fallback scan, and extended
[!] $bitcoin$ fixture lines. Full lines with encrypted ckey + pubkey can
[!] save PRIV/PUBKEY; mkey-only lines save PASSWORD/MKEY only.

[!] Enable:
[!] -walletdat FILE1 FILE2 ...     Load multiple wallet.dat files.
[!] -walletdat -f DIR              Recursively load *.dat files.
[!] -walletdat -scan-all -f DIR    Deep scan every file in the tree for wallet-like
[!]                                records and $bitcoin$ hash lines.

[!] Candidate sources:
[!] -i FILE                        Dictionary/password list, repeatable.
[!] -hex                           Dictionary lines are hex-encoded password bytes.
[!] -mask MASK                     GPU mask brute force.
[!] -mask-file FILE                One mask per line.
[!] -start HEX [-end HEX]          Raw byte range, length-major, up to 127 bytes.
[!] -n N                           Optional generated-candidate launch window.
[!]                                Too-small windows are raised to keep expensive
[!]                                wallet.dat KDF chunks GPU-filled.
[!] -wallet-load-only              Parse/load targets and exit before GPU brute-force.
[!] -walletdat-max-ckey N          Raw parser ckey scan cap per wallet file.
[!]                                Default 8 for fast multi-wallet preload; 0 = unlimited scan.
[!] -walletdat-kdf-work N          Soft max summed KDF iterations per GPU target chunk.
[!]                                Default 1000000; chunks keep at least 64 targets
[!]                                where possible to avoid underfilled GPU launches.
[!] -walletdat-max-iter N          Optional speed cap: skip compact mkey targets above N
[!]                                iterations. Default 0 = full coverage, no cap.
[!] -walletdat-min-jobs N          Minimum password-target jobs per launch.
[!]                                Default 0 = auto capped occupancy, max 131072.
[!]                                Raise it only if extra VRAM/latency is acceptable.
[!] -walletdat-kdf-loop N          Internal staged KDF iterations per kernel launch.
[!]                                Default 16384; larger can be faster but updates speed less often.

[!] Save format:
[!] WALLETDAT:<wallet_file>:PASSWORD:<password>:PRIV:<private64hex>:PUBKEY:<pubkeyhex>
[!] WALLETDAT:<wallet_file>:PASSWORD:<password>:MKEY:<mkey_hash>:PROFILE:bitcoin-core-mkey-only

[!] Examples:
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -walletdat wallet1.dat wallet2.dat -i passwords.txt -save
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -walletdat -f wallets -mask "?a?a?a?a?a?a?a?a" -save
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -walletdat -scan-all -f wallets -i passwords.txt -save

[!] ======================================================================
[!] MAIN MODE: -browservault  (Browser extension / desktop vault recovery)
[!] ======================================================================
[!] Purpose:
[!] Recover passwords for browser extension vault JSON blobs.
[!] Loader scans .ldb/.log/.json/.txt/no-extension files and extracts:
[!] - MetaMask/browser-passworder data/iv/salt vaults.
[!] - Phantom encryptedMnemonic/encryptedKey vaults.
[!] - Atomic Wallet CryptoJS/OpenSSL Salted__ AES vaults.
[!] GPU worker verifies PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 + AES-256-GCM for MetaMask,
[!] PBKDF2/scrypt + XSalsa20-Poly1305 secretbox for Phantom,
[!] and MD5 EVP_BytesToKey + AES-256-CBC for Atomic.

[!] Enable:
[!] -browservault FILE1 FILE2 ...  Load vault-containing files.
[!] -browservault -f DIR           Recursively scan browser storage/export files.

[!] Candidate sources:
[!] -i FILE                        Dictionary/password list, repeatable.
[!] -hex                           Dictionary lines are hex-encoded password bytes.
[!] -mask MASK                     GPU mask brute force.
[!] -mask-file FILE                One mask per line.
[!] -start HEX [-end HEX]          Raw byte range, length-major, up to 127 bytes.
[!] -n N                           Optional generated-candidate launch window.
[!]                                Dictionary mode may raise too-small -n to keep
[!]                                PBKDF2/ECDH chunks GPU-filled.
[!] -wallet-scrypt-mem MiB         Optional Phantom scrypt scratch cap per GPU.
[!]                                Higher values can improve speed at higher VRAM cost.
[!]                                On 24GB GPUs: 16384 is safe, 20480 is faster if no huge filters are loaded.

[!] Save format:
[!] BROWSERVAULT:<file>:PASSWORD:<password>:VAULT:<sha256>:PROFILE:<profile>

[!] Example:
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -browservault -f browser_data -i passwords.txt -save

