[!] ======================================================================
[!] MAIN MODE: -priv
[!] ======================================================================
[!] Purpose:
[!] Private key mode with mutation/bruteforce/hash families.

[!] Enable:
[!] -priv                          basic priv mode.

[!] Submodes:
[!] -priv 0                        256-bit circular left rotation combinations.
[!] -priv 1                        For each selected byte: brute 00..FF.
[!]     -pb LIST                   Byte positions for mode 1.
[!]                                Example: -pb 1,2,3-10 -- defaults 1-32.
[!] -priv 2                        Random byte change combinations.
[!] -priv 3                        16 sha256 + 16 keccak variants per key.
[!] -priv 4                        +1 for each next byte pattern.
[!] -priv 5                        +1 over all bytes (256-style sweep).
[!] -priv 6                        Repeat first N hex symbols over the full 64-hex key.
[!]     -pb LIST                   Pattern length list/range(s), example: -pb 1,2,3-6.
[!]     -last                      For each generated key: cycle last hex symbol (+0..+15, wrap f->0).
[!] -priv -recovery                Recover 64-hex PVK templates where '*' marks unknown nibbles.
[!]                                Reads one template per line from -i FILE or STDIN.

[!] Detailed flags for -priv:
[!] File input:
[!] -i FILE                        Private-key input file. STDIN is used when -i is not set.
[!] -hex                           ON: input line = 64 hex chars. OFF: raw 32-byte flow.
[!] -round N                       Plus/minus rounds around each generated key.
[!] -keys N                        Keys per chunk; rounded up to 1024 steps. Default: 1024.
[!] -chunk N                       Chunks per thread. Default: 1.
[!] -scalar                        Treat 32-byte input as ed25519 scalar.
[!] -LE                            With -scalar: scalar input is little-endian.
[!] -shash                         Treat 32-byte input as ed25519 hash(seed pre-clamp).
[!]
[!] Sequential private-key mode:
[!] -start HEX                     First private key in hex.
[!] -end HEX                       Last private key in hex.
[!] -step HEX                      Step between private keys, default 1.
[!] -back                          Walk backward from -start.
[!] -both                          Without -end: walk both directions around -start.
[!]                                With -start/-end: + starts at -start, - starts at -end.
[!] -random                        With -start/-end, choose random points inside that range.
[!] -n N                           Seq-random span/check window.
[!] -legacy                        Use legacy sequential/random logic.
[!] -em                            Enable secp256k1 endomorphism for -priv seq/random.
[!]
[!] PRNG mode:
[!] -prng                          Use internal 32-bit PRNG backend.
[!] -prng64                        Use internal 64-bit PRNG backend.
[!] -gen LIST                      Generator IDs list/range.
[!] -mode LIST                     Byte extraction mode IDs list/range.
[!] -byte LIST                     Generated byte sizes list/range.
[!] -shift LIST                    Skip counts list/range.
[!] -s HEX                         PRNG start seed.
[!] -e HEX                         PRNG end seed.
[!]
[!] Hexset sequential mode:
[!] -hexset HEXDIGITS              Hex alphabet, 1..16 unique nibbles in user order.
[!] -start HEX                     Required even-length hex lower bound.
[!] -end HEX                       Optional even-length hex upper bound.
[!]                                Base -priv only; not supported in -priv 0..6 or -priv -recovery.
[!]
[!] NOTE:
[!] -d derivations is not used in -priv mode.

[!] Examples:
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -priv -hex -i keys_hex.txt -c cuspex -bf bloom.blf
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -priv -start 1 -end FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF -step 1 -c cuspex
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -priv 6 -pb 3-6 -c c -bf bloom.blf
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -priv 6 -pb 2 -last -c c -bf bloom.blf
[!] ./METAL_CRYPTO_TOOLKIT -priv -recovery -i pvk_templates.txt -c cuspex -bf bloom.blf

[!] ======================================================================
[!] MAIN MODE: -xp  (XP/CryptGenRandom/OpenSSL replay)
[!] ======================================================================
[!] Purpose:
[!] Replay article-derived XP CryptGenRandom / OpenSSL boundary checks.
[!] Candidate generation is GPU-side only: CPU does not generate or upload priv32 lists.
[!] Important:
[!] cgr-* and ssleay-stir are article-derived trace replay profiles.
[!] openssl:* is a legacy experimental candidate family and is not proof of
[!] complete Windows XP/OpenSSL state reconstruction.
[!] Profanity is not an XP profile; use the dedicated -profanity mode.
[!]
[!] Enable:
[!] -xp PROFILE                      PROFILE is required. No implicit legacy scan.
[!]
[!] Profiles:
[!] openssl, openssl:all             Legacy experimental OpenSSL path scan.
[!]                                  Must be selected explicitly; not article-exact.
[!] openssl:0.3.24
[!] openssl:0.8.6-d
[!] openssl:0.8.6-qt
[!] openssl:0.9.1-d
[!] openssl:0.9.4-d
[!] openssl:path0..path53
[!] openssl:unknownA..unknownE0x     Aliases for unknown/fuzz paths 5..53.
[!] Article-derived exact profiles:
[!] cgr-state                        Input lines: state20:aux20 or label:state20:aux20.
[!]                                  Exact provider block: xval=state20+aux20,
[!]                                  SHA1Compress(xval||44 zero bytes), 2 blocks -> output32.
[!]                                  Also supports -f DIR source scan for article/repo
[!]                                  artifacts containing state20_before + aux20_final.
[!] cgr-bridge                       Input lines: state20:sysfunc036_raw20:outbuf_prefix20
[!]                                  or label:state20:sysfunc036_raw20:outbuf_prefix20.
[!]                                  aux20 = sysfunc036_raw20 XOR outbuf_prefix20, then cgr-state.
[!] cgr-xor                          Input lines: state20:local_before:src20.
[!]                                  Provider-local XOR alias: aux20 = local_before XOR src20.
[!] cgr-chain                        Input lines: state20:aux20[:aux20...], up to 4 transitions.
[!]                                  Saves output32 from the last provider transition.
[!] cgr-chain-bridge                 Input lines: state20:raw20:prefix20[:raw20:prefix20...].
[!]                                  Up to 4 V29-style aux20 = raw20 XOR prefix20 transitions.
[!] ssleay-stir                      Input: ssleay_stir_randbytes_trace.jsonl.
[!]                                  Replays post-stir OpenSSL RAND_bytes SHA1 stages to output32.
[!] raw32, randbytes                 Input lines: priv32/output32 or label:priv32/output32.
[!]                                  For observed RAND_bytes/out40[:32] corpus only.
[!]                                  Also supports -f DIR source scan of replay artifacts.
[!] milksad-bx256                    Libbitcoin bx seed historical profile.
[!] milksad-bx128, milksad-bx192     Host-side alias to -entropy -prng -gen 89 -mode 11
[!]                                  with -byte 16/24/32. Uses -s/-e seed range and -d paths.
[!] milksad-bx256-seq               Milk Sad update #13 sequential bx cluster.
[!]                                  Host-side alias to bx256 plus source-backed
[!]                                  PRNG offsets 0,32,...,576 rounds.
[!] bip3x-mingw                      bip3x 2.1.1 MinGW std::mt19937 profile.
[!] bip3x-mingw160/224/288/320       Host-side alias to -entropy -prng -gen 89 -mode 11
[!]                                  with -byte 20/28/36/40. Use milksad-bx128/192/256
[!]                                  for exact 16/24/32-byte bx seed widths.
[!] milksad-bx-ecnew                 Milk Sad #1 Libbitcoin bx ec-new BIP32.
[!]                                  Host-side alias to -bip32 6 -prng -gen 89
[!]                                  -mode 11 -byte 16,24,32,64. Defaults to m/
[!]                                  when -d is not supplied.
